    "rawMarkdown": "# Part One · Salaam\n\n*Late August to early October. A season of first words.*\n\n## Episode 1 · The Afternoon of the Invitation\n\n*Wednesday, 19 August, to Saturday, 22 August · Now playing: a piano, from another room*\n\nFate, Waqas would decide later, had a sense of timing no drama writer would dare to use.\n\nIt began as an ordinary afternoon. A family’s invitation travelled from one home to another, carried by phone calls, good intentions and the careful pauses of people choosing their words. Then the afternoon went quiet. An hour passed, then a few more. Somewhere in those hours, in a house he had never seen, a family weighed a question and came to an answer: yes. They would visit.\n\nThere was nothing dramatic about the answer when it arrived. It was simply the first opening in a story that neither family could yet see. Later, Waqas would remember the speed with which the visit was arranged, and how much had begun with that one yes.\n\nNobody told Waqas that his life had just turned a corner. Nobody ever does. He heard it as news about a name and a family, and he went on with his evening, and he did not yet know to be nervous.\n\nThe next day, Thursday, a little before Maghrib, an aunt walked into the room holding a phone. She had the composed expression of someone bringing news she already believed would matter.\n\n“Look,” she said.\n\nHe looked.\n\nA photograph. Light green clothes. A smile.\n\nHe had prepared a sensible response for this exact moment. It was the kind of thing a grown man says when he is being careful: Let me think about it. Let me see what everyone says. We’ll see. He had rehearsed it, on and off, for days.\n\nWhat actually left his mouth was one word.\n\n“Alhamdulillah.”\n\nNot wow. Not she’s pretty. Thanks first. In his experience, that was how you knew a thing was serious: when the first feeling was not excitement but gratitude.\n\nThe aunt smiled the smile of a woman who has been proven right.\n\nBy eight that same evening, Shajer’s father and her uncle had come to meet him.\n\nTwo hours, from photograph to doorbell. The visit had happened quickly. Waqas was still trying to take in the photograph when the family arrived, and the evening seemed to have moved ahead of him.\n\nRare, Waqas thought, sitting up straighter than he had ever sat in his life. Fast. It feels as if fate is in a hurry.\n\nTwo days later, on Saturday at six, there was a second visit, and with it a second photograph. Dark green this time. There was a hint of nerves in the picture, but she was smiling.\n\nShe looked as beautiful as he had imagined. He had, after all, been imagining generously.\n\nHe still had not heard her voice. He did not have her number. He did not know whether she liked her tea sweet, or sang in the shower, or hated mornings.\n\nNone of that seemed to matter. A decision had begun to form inside him, quietly and without asking permission.\n"
