    "summary": "On engagement day, before even knowing her voice or her favourite song, Waqas makes a calm, permanent choice in a quiet corner: I am not letting go of her hand.",
    "imageKey": null,
    "rawMarkdown": "## Episode 2 · The Decision Before the Knowing\n\n*Saturday, 29 August · Now playing: a guitar, unhurried*\n\nThey were engaged in the morning.\n\nThere were relatives, sweets, photographs and blessings, and the happy noise of a house full of people. On Shajer’s hand, Waqas would learn later, there was mehndi, dark and new, the kind of detail that makes a day feel permanent.\n\nBy late afternoon the house had emptied. A little before five, Waqas found a corner where the noise could not follow and sat down with the strange feeling of having been handed something precious before he knew how to hold it.\n\nHe thought about the girl in the photographs. He thought about how little he knew. He did not know her voice. He did not know her favourite song, or what she was afraid of, or how she took her tea. They had not exchanged a single word.\n\nAnd yet he knew one thing, with a certainty so calm that it surprised him.\n\nI am not letting go of her hand.\n\nNot I might like her. Not let us see how it goes. Just that: a plain sentence, set down in a quiet chest, without any music.\n\nLater, when he learned that her favourite film was Saiyara, he would smile at the strangeness of it. He had already decided to be the boy who stays. He had made that choice before he knew the story had a name for it.\n\nIn that corner, Waqas understood that his decision had arrived before his knowledge. He still knew almost nothing about her. He only knew that he wanted to keep choosing her. A feeling might change with the day; a choice had to be made again, and he intended to make it.\n\nThat night, a little after midnight, he found her name and pressed Follow.\n\nHe put the phone down, picked it up again, and checked whether she had accepted. He checked more than once through the night, until Fajr found him with the phone in one hand and a prayer mat in the other.\n\nIt was accepted the next day.\n\nHe was, he would later write, far too happy about it for a grown man.\n"
