    "summary": "At 5:19 PM, Waqas sends his carefully drafted first greeting, receives her unforgettable Walaikum asalam absolutely, and experiences her shy vanishing and next-day return.",
    "imageKey": null,
    "rawMarkdown": "## Episode 3 · The First Word\n\n*Monday, 31 August · Now playing: a notification tone, slightly too loud*\n\nThe message took a long time to write, and it was meant to look as if it had taken none.\n\nThat was the whole art of it. It had to sound easy, a little playful, as if he were sending his first word to his new fiancée between two more important things. Waqas wrote it, deleted it, wrote it again, made it shorter, made it warmer, then changed it once more. He realised he was treating a greeting like a legal document.\n\nAt 5:19 in the evening, he pressed send.\n\n> Assalamu alaikum.. 😊 i’m texting you assuming we know each other.. is that true..? 😇\n\nThen came the three small words everyone learns to dread: Sent. Delivered. Seen.\n\nThe dots appeared. They vanished. They appeared again, and he held his breath long enough to feel it.\n\n> Walaikum asalam absolutely.\n\nAbsolutely.\n\nHe read it once, then again, quite calmly. It was a single word, and it made his careful question feel unnecessary. Did they know each other? They had no conversations behind them, only two photographs and a great many opinions from aunts, and she had answered as if the answer were obvious. As if it had been waiting for someone to ask.\n\nThey exchanged three or four more messages. Small ones, polite, wonderful.\n\nAnd then she was gone.\n\nWaqas stared at the chat, waiting for it to resume. It did not.\n\nHe tried to be sensible. People had lives. People got busy. A person could not be expected to sit beside a phone because somebody else had finally found the courage to say hello. He told himself all this in a calm voice, and then he checked the phone again.\n\nBy nine o’clock he had produced three theories. She was busy. Her phone had died. She had read his messages, thought oh no, this is not what I imagined, and quietly closed the door.\n\nThe third theory was, naturally, the one he believed.\n\nAt her end, Waqas can only guess, and she is welcome to correct him. Perhaps the first exchange had been enough for one evening. Perhaps she needed a little room to breathe. He would later learn that opening up was difficult for her.\n\nHe could only imagine that she had been brave for those first messages and then needed to step back.\n\nIt was, he would come to think, entirely in character for the shyest person in the world. That was his affectionate description, not a claim that he could know what she had thought that evening.\n\nHe did not know any of that yet. That night he slept badly. In the morning, he tried to leave his phone alone for a while. It did not last.\n\nThe next day, a little after noon, the screen lit up.\n\n> Assalamu alaikum, khairiyat?\n\nSome messages look ordinary to the rest of the world and become landmarks to the person receiving them. This was one. She had come back. More than that, she had begun the conversation herself.\n\nWaqas read it, smiled, and tried to look unmoved. It is difficult to look composed when you are alone in a room and very happy.\n\nHe answered in under four seconds, which he regretted at once.\n\nThat was how it began: with a vanishing and a return. The first Salaam was his. The next one, when it mattered, was hers.\n\nHe did not know it yet, but Assalamu alaikum would become the doorway of their whole story. The greeting would remain familiar even as more words gradually found their way between them.\n"
