    "imageKey": null,
    "rawMarkdown": "## Episode 4 · Did You Eat?\n\n*September · Now playing: soft guitar, a kettle humming in the background*\n\nThey did not leap into each other. That was the first thing that was right about them.\n\nThere were no long confessions, no speeches and no pretending to have known each other for years. There were small steps: a question, an answer, a pause, another question. They were learning the pace of one another without trying to hurry it.\n\nOn the fourth of September, they exchanged photographs.\n\nIt was a simple thing, and it felt enormous. Until then, each had been assembling the other from fragments: a name, a family story, a greeting, a few typed sentences and photographs passed between relatives. This one was different. It was sent on purpose, from her to him and from him to her. A face had finally been attached to the words. Waqas looked at it for a long time. For once, he did not try to think of something clever to say.\n\nAt first, messages did most of the work. There were voice notes and brief calls as the weeks went on, but conversation took longer to arrive. Neither of them seemed in a hurry to force it.\n\nIt was not a formal rule. It was simply the pace they found: careful, respectful, and their own.\n\nSomewhere in those first weeks, Waqas began to ask a question.\n\nThe question became a familiar part of their exchange.\n\nHave you eaten?\n\nHe asked it the way it had always been asked in his world: at the door, at the end of a call, by his Ammi, by the aunts, by everyone who loved someone and did not have the vocabulary for it. In the house where he grew up, have you eaten? was never only about food. It meant: Are you well? Have you looked after yourself? Did anyone remember to ask? Is there something I can do, even from here?\n\nAt first it was simple. Had she eaten? Was she okay? Had her day been tiring? But a question asked with care, and asked again, can become a ritual. And a ritual can become a kind of shelter.\n\nAs the weeks passed, Waqas began to understand that the question carried more than its literal meaning.\n\nThe dramas Shajer enjoyed had given him another way to recognise it: in those stories, asking whether someone had eaten could be a quiet way of saying that you cared. Waqas did not need to turn the question into a speech. He kept asking it.\n\nThe words were ordinary. The care inside them was not.\n\nHe began to notice how often he reached for the phone with that one question in mind.\n\n> Khana khaya?\n\nHe kept asking, after that. The only difference was that he had begun to understand how much the question could hold.\n"
