    "rawMarkdown": "## Episode 6 · Munnar, Mehndi, and a Voice\n\n*Sunday, 13 September, to Wednesday, 16 September · Now playing: wind through tea hills*\n\nThe hills of Munnar were beautiful: green beyond the eye’s ability to measure, clouds drifting as if they had nowhere to be, roads winding away into mist. Waqas had gone somewhere lovely and found, with some astonishment, that he was missing someone who was not there.\n\nIt surprised him. He had known Shajer for only a short while. How could an absence already have a shape?\n\nBut he did. He missed her messages and the small habits of her day arriving on his screen. He missed the person who had begun to occupy the spaces between one task and the next, so gently that he had not noticed until he was away.\n\nThe mist rolled over the valley. He stood in front of one of the most beautiful views he had seen and thought about his phone.\n\nThe next day, a photograph reached him: her hand, and on it the fading mehndi from their engagement.\n\nIt was temporary, but it brought the day back to him. The promise had been made. The colour would fade; what they had promised was meant to last.\n\nOn the fifteenth, he woke to a voice note.\n\nHer first.\n\nAssalamu alaikum wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuhu.\n\nHe listened to a greeting he had read a hundred times become, suddenly, a person. It had breath and softness. It belonged to the morning in a way a typed message could not.\n\nShe had used the long form, the full blessing: peace be upon you, and the mercy of Allah, and His blessings. It was the complete greeting, not the short Salaam he knew from their messages.\n\nHe played it again. And again. His own voice was still something he was working up the courage to send.\n\nThe next day they found another way to be together.\n\nIt began with stone-paper-scissors, argued over text with the gravity of an international tournament. Then, because that was clearly not enough, Ludo. Waqas won the first round. Shajer won the second, and celebrated with a run of lucky rolls and a single crown emoji.\n\nHe accepted his defeat gracefully for almost four seconds.\n\nNeither of them could have said who was winning overall. It hardly mattered. They had found another way to spend time in each other’s company, and both were happy either way.\n\nThat, Waqas thought later, was a good sign. They could lose a game and keep smiling.\n"
