    "rawMarkdown": "## Episode 24 · Midnight\n\n*Thursday, 31 December, night · Now playing: the sound of a house, finally asleep*\n\nThere is a curious fact, one every family in Ambur could have predicted: at their own wedding, the bride and groom are often the only two people who do not get to eat.\n\nThey were photographed, blessed, embraced and asked to hold sweets for a great many cameras. Everyone said the biryani was the best they had ever tasted. And the two of them, who had asked each other khana khaya? every day for months, forgot to eat on the one day when it mattered most.\n\nIt was past ten by the time the house grew quiet. They were alone at last, in a room with the day folding itself away. Waqas sat on the edge of the bed. Shajer sat beside him, her dupatta around her shoulders and her hennaed hands in her lap.\n\n“Khana khaya?” he said, because he could not help himself.\n\n“No,” said Shajer.\n\n“Neither did I.”\n\nThey looked at each other. And then both of them started to laugh, helplessly, in the way that you laugh when you are too tired and too happy to do anything else.\n\nAmmi, naturally, had thought of it. Behind the door sat a steel tiffin wrapped in a newspaper and towel. Biryani, still warm. On the lid was a note in Ammi’s unmistakable handwriting: Eat.\n\nThere was one plate, and there were two spoons, and a long grave debate about who would get the last piece of chicken, which Shajer won.\n\nIt was, he decided, the best meal of his life.\n\nAfter Isha, they prayed together. The room grew quiet afterwards, and the gratitude between them needed no speech.\n\nWhen it was over, he took out his phone.\n\n“What are you doing?” she said.\n\n“There are two pages left.”\n\nHe opened the app and took her to the page called Nikkahnama, with its two circles and its line of Arabic.\n\n> Place and hold your thumb on your designated scanner, together, to seal.\n\nShe looked at the screen, and then at him.\n\nShe put her thumb on the left circle. He put his on the right. The phone lit beneath their hands, and the page filled with gold.\n\n> Sealed.\n\nNeither of them said a word. They did not need to.\n\n“And the other one,” said Shajer, softly.\n\nHe knew which she meant. The Lovenama page was still blank. He had written its instruction long ago: this page was for the moment of love, when neither person needed to guess. He had promised not to fill it before that moment was real.\n\nHe turned the phone towards her. The blank page remained between them, not as an omission but as a space they could leave for another day. They did not sign it.\n\nLater, when the house had finally fallen quiet, they stood together by the window. The clock moved towards midnight. There was no scene to perform and no music to wait for.\n\n“Assalamu alaikum,” said Shajer softly, into the first minute of the year.\n\n“Walaikum assalam, ruhi,” said Waqas.\n\nIt was the same greeting that had begun their story. It had not changed. The life around it had.\n"
