    "rawMarkdown": "# Epilogue · Still Writing\n\n*Friday, 1 January 2027 · Fajr · Now playing: a piano, from another room, slowly, and then a little faster*\n\nThe phone did not ring.\n\nIt was the first morning in four months that it had not. No three rings at five. No ruhi.. ❤️ glowing in the dark. No lock screen stacked with missed calls, no sleepy Salaam, no half-minute of breathing across a hundred and ninety kilometres. The phone lay on the table, face down and quiet. Its work had changed.\n\nWaqas woke anyway.\n\nHe woke in a room he was still learning. The light was grey. The year had turned in the night, and the house was quiet.\n\nAnd then, close, in a voice that had no line in it, no static, no distance, only the plain warm air of a room, someone said:\n\n“Wake up, Jaana.”\n\nHe turned his head.\n\nShajer was sitting at the edge of the bed with her hair loose and her dupatta around her shoulders, already wudu-fresh and bright-eyed. She looked at him with an expression he could not quite name.\n\nHer voice sounded different without a phone between them. It was also the same.\n\n“I was already awake,” said Waqas.\n\n“Liar.”\n\n“I was thinking about it.”\n\n“About what?”\n\n“About how nobody had to call.”\n\nShe laughed softly, and the grey room got warmer.\n\nThey prayed Fajr together. When it was over, and the light at the curtain had begun to change, he opened the project on his phone. There was one more thing to write.\n\nHe scrolled down to the end of the Timeline, where the last card had been waiting since November.\n\n> Still Writing. More memories coming, starting with the thirty-first of December.\n\nHe smiled. He had never changed that card. It had never needed changing. But now he added two above it.\n\n> 31 Dec. 11:30 AM. Qubool Hai.\n> 1 Jan. Fajr. Nobody had to call.\n\nAnd then, for the last time that morning, he went back to the final card, and with his thumb he changed one word.\n\n> Still Writing. More memories coming, starting today.\n\nThe first Tuesday arrived on the fifth of January, as Tuesdays do.\n\nIt was an ordinary day. Waqas was late home. Shajer was tired. The tea had gone cold on the counter, and a small, unnecessary disagreement had grown out of a long day. Neither could afterwards remember exactly what had started it.\n\nThen one of them looked at the clock.\n\n“Sone se pehle,” said Shajer.\n\n“Pakka,” said Waqas.\n\nThey sorted it out before sleep. It took a little time, one fresh cup of tea and the willingness to apologise before pride could get in the way.\n\nBefore he put the light out, he opened the project one last time. He added a card.\n\n> 5 Jan. Tuesday. Nothing happened. Everything did.\n\nHe showed it to her.\n\nShe read it slowly, with her chin on his shoulder, with the lamp making a small gold circle on the page. She did not say anything for a long time.\n\n“Nobody shows the Tuesdays,” she said at last.\n\n“We do,” said Waqas.\n\nOn the screen, beneath the new card, a small line of text glowed in the dark:\n\n> Still Writing.\n"
